.TH "AUTOWT" "1" "May 2026" "autowt Manual"
.SH NAME
autowt \- git worktree manager with terminal switching and cleanup
.SH COMMAND HELP
For command options, run \f[CR]autowt \-\-help\f[R] or
\f[CR]autowt <command> \-\-help\f[R].
.SH autowt: a better git worktree experience
.SS What are worktrees?
\c
.UR https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree
Worktrees
.UE \c
\ are a built\-in feature of git, which are essentially free clones of a
local git repo.
History is shared and synced across all worktrees for a given repo.
Creating a new worktree is cheap, and you can list all your worktrees
with a single command.
This makes them a great fit for doing work \(lqin parallel,\(rq or not
worrying about having uncommitted changes before working on another
branch.
.SS How autowt simplifies common workflows
While worktrees are powerful, the built\-in tooling is minimalistic.
Consider what it takes to set up a fresh worktree in a typical workflow:
.IP "1." 3
Make a decision about where to put the worktree
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]git worktree add <worktree_path> \-b <branch>\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
Open a new terminal tab
.IP "4." 3
\f[CR]cd <worktree path>\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
\f[CR]uv sync\f[R] or \f[CR]npm install\f[R] or whatever your dependency
setup is
.IP "6." 3
\f[CR]cp <repo_dir>/.env .\f[R] to copy secrets
.PP
Congrats, you\(cqre done!
Type type type, open a PR, and merge it.
Now you need to clean up:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]git worktree rm .\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
Close the tab
.PP
On the other hand, \f[B]with autowt, it looks like this:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
autowt <branch>
.EE
.PP
And deleting branches that have been merged or are associated with
closed PRs looks like this:
.IP
.EX
autowt cleanup
.EE
.PP
A lot nicer, right?
.PP
Now suppose your team uses an issue tracker like Linear which can
suggest branch names based on issue IDs.
You could configure autowt to have a custom command to automatically
open worktrees for tickets instead of passing a branch name:
.IP
.EX
autowt linear ABC\-1234 \f[I]# opens yourname/abc\-1234\-title\-of\-the\-ticket or whatever\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Note:
.PP
This example mentions Linear, but autowt has no opinions about which
tools you call in your scripts.
There is no special GitHub or Linear integration.
That functionality comes from command line programs installed and
configured by you.
.SS What autowt can do for you
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B] Worktree ergonomics\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
It\(cqs not hard to learn the commands to manage worktrees, but autowt
shortens the most common ones.
autowt can open new terminal tabs via \c
.UR https://github.com/irskep/automate-terminal
automate\-terminal
.UE \c
\ (iTerm2, tmux, Ghostty, and more), or with shell integration it can
\f[CR]cd\f[R] directly in your current shell.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B] Deep, customizable automation\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
You can define scripts in \f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R] to run at various
points, like after creating a worktree but before switching to it, or
before a worktree is cleaned up.
Check out Lifecycle Hooks for more information.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B] Smart cleanup\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
You can configure autowt to automatically clean up worktrees whose
branches have been merged, or even branches which are associated with
closed pull requests on GitHub.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B] Friendly TUIs\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
autowt uses interactive terminal\-based UIs where it makes sense.
For example, \f[CR]autowt config\f[R] gives you an easy way to edit
global settings.
\f[CR]autowt switch\f[R] lets you review your worktrees and pick which
one to navigate to.
.RE
.SS Getting started
You\(cqll need \f[CR]git\f[R] 2.5+ (anything from the last ten years).
.PP
Install autowt with Homebrew:
.IP
.EX
brew tap irskep/tap
brew trust irskep/tap
brew install autowt
.EE
.PP
Optionally, set up shell integration so worktree switches \f[CR]cd\f[R]
in your current shell:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# bash/zsh (add to \(ti/.bashrc or \(ti/.zshrc)\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init bash)\(dq
.EE
.PP
Then, make a new worktree for a new or existing branch in your current
repo:
.IP
.EX
autowt my\-new\-feature
.EE
.PP
With shell integration, this creates the worktree and \f[CR]cd\f[R]\(cqs
you into it.
Without it, autowt opens a new terminal tab or window instead.
.SH Installing autowt
Note:
.PP
Before you begin, make sure you have at least git 2.5.
.SS Install autowt
Homebrew is the recommended installation method for most users:
.IP
.EX
brew tap irskep/tap
brew trust irskep/tap
brew install autowt
.EE
.PP
Verify the installation:
.IP
.EX
autowt \-\-version
awt \-\-version
.EE
.PP
Homebrew installs both \f[CR]autowt\f[R] and \f[CR]awt\f[R].
.SS Organization\-managed install with mise
If you want everyone working in a repository to use the same autowt
toolchain, install autowt through mise\(cqs GitHub backend:
.IP
.EX
mise use github:irskep/autowt
.EE
.PP
Then run:
.IP
.EX
mise install
.EE
.PP
You can also install it globally with mise:
.IP
.EX
mise use \-g github:irskep/autowt
.EE
.PP
The mise install path provides \f[CR]autowt\f[R].
If you want the shorter \f[CR]awt\f[R] command with mise, install shell
integration; it defines \f[CR]awt\f[R] as an alias or function.
.SS Shell integration (optional)
By default, autowt opens new terminal tabs when you switch worktrees.
If you\(cqd prefer worktree switches to \f[CR]cd\f[R] in your current
shell, add shell integration to your shell config:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# bash (\(ti/.bashrc)\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init bash)\(dq

\f[I]# zsh (\(ti/.zshrc)\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init zsh)\(dq

\f[I]# fish (\(ti/.config/fish/config.fish)\f[R]
autowt shell\-init fish \f[B]|\f[R] source
.EE
.PP
See Terminal Support for details.
.SH Getting started with autowt
This guide will walk you through installing \f[CR]autowt\f[R], setting
it up for a project, and using its core features to streamline your
development workflow.
.SS Your first worktree
Let\(cqs dive in and see \f[CR]autowt\f[R] in action.
.SS Step 1: Create a new feature branch
Navigate to the root of any git repository you\(cqre working on.
For this example, let\(cqs say your project is located at
\f[CR]\(ti/dev/my\-project\f[R].
.IP
.EX
cd \(ti/dev/my\-project
.EE
.PP
Now, let\(cqs create a worktree for a new feature.
.IP
.EX
autowt go new\-feature
.EE
.PP
Here\(cqs what \f[CR]autowt\f[R] does behind the scenes:
.IP "1." 3
Fetches the latest changes from your remote repository.
.IP "2." 3
Creates a new directory for your worktree at
\f[CR]../my\-project\-worktrees/new\-feature/\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
Creates a new git worktree for the \f[CR]new\-feature\f[R] branch.
If the branch doesn\(cqt exist, it will be created from your main
branch.
.IP "4." 3
Navigates to the new worktree directory (either by opening a new
terminal tab, or \f[CR]cd\f[R]\(cqing in your current shell if you\(cqve
set up shell integration).
.PP
You now have a clean, isolated environment for your new feature, without
disturbing the main branch.
.SS Step 2: List your worktrees
To see an overview of your worktrees, use the \f[CR]ls\f[R] command:
.IP
.EX
autowt ls
.EE
.PP
The output will look something like this, with an arrow \f[CR]→\f[R]
indicating your current directory and a \f[CR]\(at\f[R] icon for active
terminal sessions.
.IP
.EX
  Worktrees:
→ \(ti/dev/my\-project\-worktrees/new\-feature \(at      new\-feature ←
  \(ti/dev/my\-project (main worktree)                     main
.EE
.PP
Info:
.PP
\f[CR]autowt\f[R] with no arguments is an alias for
\f[CR]autowt ls\f[R].
.PP
Tip: Interactive switching
.PP
You can also use \f[CR]autowt switch\f[R] with no arguments to open an
interactive TUI for selecting an existing worktree.
.PP
Tip: Additional worktree setup
.PP
If you want dependencies to be installed automatically when creating new
worktrees, or need to copy over git\-ignored files like \f[CR].env\f[R]
from the main worktree, you can learn how to configure lifecycle hooks
in the Lifecycle Hooks guide.
.SS A typical workflow
Now that you have the basics down, let\(cqs walk through a common
development scenario.
.SS Juggling multiple tasks
Imagine you\(cqre working on \f[CR]new\-feature\f[R] when you get a
request for an urgent bug fix.
With \f[CR]autowt\f[R], you don\(cqt need to stash your changes.
Just create a new worktree for the hotfix:
.IP
.EX
autowt hotfix/urgent\-bug
.EE
.PP
autowt switches you to the bug fix worktree.
You can now work on the fix without affecting your
\f[CR]new\-feature\f[R] branch.
Once you\(cqre done with the bug fix, switch back to your feature or let
\f[CR]autowt cleanup\f[R] handle it later.
.PP
If you prefer to stay in your existing terminal tab the whole time, you
can set up shell integration:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Add to your shell config once:\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init bash)\(dq  \f[I]# or zsh/fish\f[R]

\f[I]# Then just use autowt normally \(em it cd\(aqs in your current shell\f[R]
autowt hotfix/urgent\-bug
\f[I]# code code code, commit, push\f[R]
autowt new\-feature
.EE
.PP
Without shell integration, you can also pass
\f[CR]\-\-terminal=inplace\f[R] for the same effect on a one\-off basis
using AppleScript or dbus to \(lqtype\(rq into the current terminal.
.PP
For special cases where you need the worktree in a specific location,
you can override the default directory pattern with \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Place in a custom location\f[R]
autowt urgent\-fix \-\-dir /tmp/quick\-fix

\f[I]# Use a relative path from current directory\f[R]
autowt feature\-demo \-\-dir ../demo\-workspace
.EE
.PP
Run \f[CR]autowt config\f[R] to configure the default terminal behavior
for switching worktrees.
.SS Cleaning up
Once your \f[CR]hotfix/urgent\-bug\f[R] branch is merged and no longer
needed, you can clean it up.
.PP
First, use the \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] flag to see what
\f[CR]autowt\f[R] will do:
.IP
.EX
autowt cleanup \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
This will show you a list of branches that are safe to remove.
When you\(cqre ready, run the command without the flag:
.IP
.EX
autowt cleanup
.EE
.PP
On first run, \f[CR]autowt\f[R] will ask you to select your preferred
cleanup mode (interactive, merged, remoteless, or github if the GitHub
CLI is available).
Your choice will be saved for future use.
After that, \f[CR]autowt\f[R] will remove the worktree and, if the
branch is merged, will also offer to delete the local git branch.
.PP
If \f[CR]autowt cleanup\f[R] doesn\(cqt want to automatically clean up
your branch, you can run \f[CR]autowt cleanup <branch\-name>\f[R]
explicitly.
.SH CLI guide
This page explains the main command\-line workflows.
For exact flags, aliases, and generated Cobra help, use the Command
Reference or run \f[CR]autowt <command> \-\-help\f[R].
.SS Command forms
Homebrew installs two equivalent binary names:
.IP
.EX
autowt ls
awt ls
.EE
.PP
The mise install path provides \f[CR]autowt\f[R]; install shell
integration if you want the shorter \f[CR]awt\f[R] command with mise.
.PP
Running \f[CR]autowt\f[R] with no arguments lists worktrees.
Running \f[CR]autowt <branch>\f[R] is shorthand for switching to a
branch or creating a worktree for it:
.IP
.EX
autowt
autowt feature/my\-change
autowt switch feature/my\-change
.EE
.PP
Use the explicit \f[CR]switch\f[R] command if a branch name conflicts
with a built\-in command name.
.SS Switch and Create
\f[CR]autowt switch [branch]\f[R] switches to an existing worktree or
creates a new one.
It accepts branch names, branch names without a configured prefix, and
paths to existing worktree directories.
With no branch argument, it opens an interactive picker for existing
worktrees.
.PP
When creating a worktree, autowt checks local branches first, then
remote branches, then offers to create a new branch from the
repository\(cqs main branch.
Use \f[CR]\-\-from\f[R] to choose a specific source revision.
.PP
Terminal behavior comes from configuration by default.
Override it per command with \f[CR]\-\-terminal\f[R], or use shell
integration when you want switching to \f[CR]cd\f[R] in the current
shell instead of opening another terminal session.
.PP
Worktree directories use \f[CR]worktree.directory_pattern\f[R].
The \f[CR]{branch}\f[R] template value is controlled by
\f[CR]worktree.flatten_worktree_directories\f[R]: \f[CR]true\f[R]
replaces branch path separators with hyphens, while \f[CR]false\f[R]
preserves them as nested directories.
.SS List
\f[CR]autowt ls\f[R] lists worktrees for the current repository.
The main worktree appears first, the current worktree is marked, and
active terminal sessions are indicated when autowt can detect them.
.IP
.EX
> autowt ls

  Worktrees:
\-> \(ti/dev/my\-project (main worktree)                         main <\-
  \(ti/dev/my\-project\-worktrees/feature\-new\-ui \(at   feature\-new\-ui
  \(ti/dev/my\-project\-worktrees/hotfix\-bug              hotfix\-bug
.EE
.SS Cleanup
\f[CR]autowt cleanup\f[R] removes secondary worktrees and can also
delete their local branches.
With no arguments it uses the configured cleanup mode, or prompts for a
default the first time you run it.
.PP
Cleanup modes:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]interactive\f[R]: choose worktrees in a TUI.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]merged\f[R]: select branches already merged into the main branch.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]remoteless\f[R]: select branches without upstream tracking.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]github\f[R]: use the GitHub CLI to select branches with merged or
closed pull requests.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]all\f[R]: combine merged and remoteless cleanup.
.PP
You can also pass branch names or worktree paths directly:
.IP
.EX
autowt cleanup feature/my\-change
autowt cleanup ../my\-project\-worktrees/feature\-my\-change
.EE
.PP
Use \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] to preview removals and \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R]
when git should remove a worktree with local file changes.
.SS Configuration
\f[CR]autowt config\f[R] opens an interactive editor for global
configuration.
\f[CR]autowt config \-\-show\f[R] prints resolved values from defaults,
config files, environment variables, and command\-line overrides.
.PP
See Configuration for every setting and its precedence.
.SS Shell Integration
\f[CR]autowt shell\-init\f[R] prints shell code for bash, zsh, or fish.
When installed, switching commands can change the current shell
directory directly.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# bash or zsh\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init)\(dq

\f[I]# fish\f[R]
autowt shell\-init fish \f[B]|\f[R] source
.EE
.PP
Commands that do not switch worktrees, such as \f[CR]ls\f[R],
\f[CR]cleanup\f[R], and \f[CR]config\f[R], continue to print normally.
.SS Hooks
\f[CR]autowt hook <hook_name>\f[R] runs the configured global and
project lifecycle hooks for the current repository and worktree.
This lets other tools reuse autowt hook configuration instead of
duplicating it.
.PP
Hook names are \f[CR]pre_create\f[R], \f[CR]post_create\f[R],
\f[CR]post_create_async\f[R], \f[CR]session_init\f[R],
\f[CR]pre_cleanup\f[R], \f[CR]post_cleanup\f[R], \f[CR]pre_switch\f[R],
and \f[CR]post_switch\f[R].
.SS Built\-In Docs
\f[CR]autowt docs\f[R] opens the built\-in documentation in a terminal
man\-page viewer when possible.
Use \f[CR]autowt docs \-\-plain\f[R] for plain text output, or
\f[CR]autowt docs \-\-roff\f[R] to print the generated roff source.
.SS Global Options
Global options can be used with any command:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-y\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R]: automatically confirm prompts.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-debug\f[R]: enable verbose debug logging.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]: show help.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-version\f[R]: print the version.
