.SH Lifecycle Hooks and Init Scripts
\f[CR]autowt\f[R] allows you to run custom commands at specific points
during worktree operations.
The motivating use cases are installing dependencies, copying secrets,
and cleaning up resources.
.SS Example: installing dependencies and copying secrets
The \f[B]\f[CB]post_create\f[B] hook\f[R] runs as a subprocess after
creating a new worktree but before switching to it.
This is the right place for setup tasks like installing dependencies and
copying files.
.SS Configuration
Set the \f[CR]scripts.post_create\f[R] key in your
\f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R] file:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dqnpm install\(dq
.EE
.SS Copying \f[CR].env\f[R] files
Worktrees start as clean checkouts, which means untracked files like
\f[CR].env\f[R] are not automatically carried over.
You can use a hook to copy these files from your main worktree.
.PP
autowt provides environment variables that make this easier, including
\f[CR]AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR\f[R] which points to the main repository
directory.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
npm install
if [ \-f \(dq$AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env\(dq ]; then
  cp \(dq$AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env\(dq .;
fi
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Installing things in the background
If your dependency installation step takes a long time, you might wish
to use \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] instead.
That way, you can get an interactive terminal without waiting for
everything to finish.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create_async = \(dq\(dq\(dq
npm install
if [ \-f \(dq$AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env\(dq ]; then
  cp \(dq$AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env\(dq .;
fi
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Quick reference
Beyond \f[CR]session_init\f[R] scripts, autowt supports 8 lifecycle
hooks that run at specific points during worktree operations:
.PP
.TS
