.TE
.PP
Note that there is a command\-line\-only \f[CR]\-\-after\-init\f[R] flag
to run additional commands after init is done.
This is for cases when you want to immediately start up a one\-off
script after the worktree has been set up.
If you find yourself repeating the same \f[CR]\-\-after\-init\f[R] arg,
consider writing a custom script.
.SS Running hooks standalone
If you use another worktree tool but want to reuse autowt\(cqs hook
configuration, you can run individual hooks with
\f[CR]autowt hook <hook_name>\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# After your tool creates a worktree, cd into it and run:\f[R]
autowt hook post_create
autowt hook session_init

\f[I]# Before your tool deletes a worktree:\f[R]
autowt hook pre_cleanup
.EE
.PP
The command auto\-detects the repository root, worktree directory, and
branch name from the current working directory.
It runs the same global + project hook cascade as the built\-in
commands.
See the command reference for details.
.SS Configuration
Project\-level and global hooks run independently and do not override
each other.
.SS Project\-level configuration
Configure hooks in your project\(cqs \f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R] file:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
pre_create = \(dq./scripts/validate\-branch.sh\(dq
post_create = \(dqnpm install && cp .env.example .env\(dq
session_init = \(dqsource .env && npm run dev\(dq
pre_cleanup = \(dq./scripts/release\-ports.sh\(dq
post_cleanup = \(dq./scripts/cleanup\-volumes.sh\(dq
pre_switch = \(dqpkill \-f \(aqnpm run dev\(aq\(dq
post_switch = \(dqnpm run dev &\(dq
.EE
.SS Global configuration
Configure hooks globally in \f[CR]\(ti/.config/autowt/config.toml\f[R]
(Linux) or
\f[CR]\(ti/Library/Application Support/autowt/config.toml\f[R] (macOS):
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Global config\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
pre_create = \(dqecho \(aqPreparing to create worktree...\(aq\(dq
pre_cleanup = \(dqecho \(aqCleaning up worktree...\(aq\(dq
post_cleanup = \(dqecho \(aqWorktree cleanup complete\(aq\(dq
.EE
.SS Environment variables and arguments
All hooks receive the following environment variables:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AUTOWT_WORKTREE_DIR\f[R]: Path to the worktree directory (always
set, even if directory doesn\(cqt exist yet or has been deleted)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR\f[R]: Path to the main repository directory
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\f[R]: Name of the branch
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AUTOWT_HOOK_TYPE\f[R]: Type of hook being executed
.PP
\f[B]Working directory behavior\f[R]: Most hooks run with the worktree
directory as their working directory.
However, \f[CR]pre_create\f[R] and \f[CR]post_cleanup\f[R] hooks run
with the \f[B]main repository directory\f[R] as their working directory,
since the worktree doesn\(cqt exist yet (\f[CR]pre_create\f[R]) or has
been deleted (\f[CR]post_cleanup\f[R]).
.SS Example script
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Using environment variables\f[R]
echo \(dqHook type: $AUTOWT_HOOK_TYPE\(dq
echo \(dqWorktree: $AUTOWT_WORKTREE_DIR\(dq
echo \(dqBranch: $AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq

cd \(dq$AUTOWT_WORKTREE_DIR\(dq
\f[I]# Do work here...\f[R]

\f[I]# Multi\-line scripts work naturally\f[R]
\f[B]for\f[R] file \f[B]in\f[R] *.txt\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]do\f[R]
    echo \(dqProcessing $file\(dq
\f[B]done\f[R]
.EE
.SS Hooks are executing using the system shell
Hook scripts are executed by passing the script text directly to the
system shell (\f[CR]/bin/sh\f[R] on Unix systems) rather than creating a
temporary file.
This is equivalent to running
\f[CR]/bin/sh \-c \(dqyour_script_here\(dq\f[R].
.PP
This execution model means:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Multi\-line scripts work naturally\f[R]\(emthe shell handles
newlines and command separation
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]All shell features are available\f[R]\(emvariables, conditionals,
loops, pipes, redirections, etc.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Shebangs are ignored\f[R]\(emsince no file is created,
\f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R] lines are treated as comments
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Cross\-platform behavior is tricky\f[R]\(emPowerShell and bash are
quite different!
GitHub issues and pull requests on this topic are welcome.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
\f[I]# This works \- shell script commands\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
echo \(dqSetting up worktree\(dq
npm install
mkdir \-p logs
\(dq\(dq\(dq

\f[I]# This works \- calls external script file (shebang will work here)\f[R]
post_create = \(dq./setup\-script.py\(dq

\f[I]# This doesn\(aqt work \- shebang is ignored, shell tries to run Python code\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys  # Shell doesn\(aqt understand this!
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
If you need to use a different programming language, create a separate
script file and call it from your hook.
The external file can use shebangs normally.
.PP
Note: Why does it work like this?
.PP
Inline hook strings are executed as \f[CR]sh \-c <script>\f[R] in the
hook\(cqs configured working directory.
This is why shell syntax, multi\-line scripts, and shebang comments
behave the way they do.
On systems without \f[CR]sh\f[R], call an external script file instead
so the operating system can use that file\(cqs interpreter association.
.SS Complete reference
.SS \f[CR]pre_create\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: Before worktree creation begins
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Execution Context\f[R]: Subprocess in main repository directory
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Pre\-flight validation, resource availability
checks, branch name validation
.PP
The \f[CR]pre_create\f[R] hook runs in the \f[B]main repository
directory\f[R] (not the worktree directory, which doesn\(cqt exist yet).
However, the \f[CR]AUTOWT_WORKTREE_DIR\f[R] environment variable is
still set to the path where the worktree will be created.
.PP
The \f[CR]pre_create\f[R] hook can \f[B]prevent worktree creation\f[R]
by exiting with a non\-zero status.
If this hook fails, autowt will completely abort worktree creation
before the worktree is created.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
pre_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
# Validate branch naming convention
if ! echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq | grep \-q \(aq\(hafeature/\f[B]\(rs|\f[R]\(habugfix/\f[B]\(rs|\f[R]\(hahotfix/\(aq; then
  echo \(dqError: Branch must start with feature/, bugfix/, or hotfix/\(dq
  exit 1
fi
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR]post_create\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: After worktree creation, before terminal switch
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Execution Context\f[R]: Subprocess in worktree directory
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: File operations, git setup, dependency
installation, configuration copying
.PP
The \f[CR]post_create\f[R] hook can \f[B]prevent terminal switching\f[R]
by exiting with a non\-zero status.
If this hook fails, autowt will abort the operation (the worktree will
exist but the terminal won\(cqt switch to it).
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
npm install
cp .env.example .env
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
The post_create hook runs as a subprocess after the worktree is created
but before switching to the terminal session.
It\(cqs ideal for:
.IP \(bu 2
Installing dependencies that don\(cqt require shell interaction
.IP \(bu 2
Setting up configuration files
.IP \(bu 2
Running git commands
.IP \(bu 2
File operations that don\(cqt need shell environment
.SS \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: After terminal switch (TAB/WINDOW modes) or before
\(enafter\-init (ECHO/INPLACE modes) \f[B]Execution Context\f[R]:
Original terminal where autowt was invoked \f[B]Use cases\f[R]:
Expensive dependency installations that don\(cqt block terminal
interactivity
.PP
The \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] hook is designed for \f[B]expensive
operations\f[R] like \f[CR]npm install\f[R], \f[CR]poetry install\f[R],
or \f[CR]bundle install\f[R] that can run while the user is already
working in the new terminal.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create_async = \(dq\(dq\(dq
npm install
npm run build
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Execution behavior varies by terminal mode:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]TAB/WINDOW/VSCODE/CURSOR modes\f[R]: Runs in the original terminal
\f[I]after\f[R] the new terminal tab/window opens.
The user can immediately start working in the new terminal while
dependencies install in the background.
The \f[CR]autowt\f[R] process waits for completion before exiting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ECHO/INPLACE modes\f[R]: Runs \f[I]before\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-after\-init\f[R] since no actual terminal switch occurs.
Ensures expensive operations complete before any after\-init commands
run.
.PP
\f[B]Failure handling\f[R]: Unlike \f[CR]pre_create\f[R] and
\f[CR]post_create\f[R], failures in this hook show a warning but
don\(cqt abort the operation.
Since the hook may run asynchronously, failures might not be detectable
until after the user is already working.
.PP
\f[B]When to use\f[R]: Use \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] for operations
that:
.IP \(bu 2
Are expensive but not critical for immediate work
.IP \(bu 2
Don\(cqt produce output the user needs to see right away
.IP \(bu 2
Can safely run while the user starts working (installing dependencies,
building assets, etc.)
.PP
\f[B]When to use \f[CB]post_create\f[B] instead\f[R]: Use the
synchronous \f[CR]post_create\f[R] hook for operations that:
.IP \(bu 2
Must complete before the user can work (copying config files, setting
permissions)
.IP \(bu 2
Are fast and shouldn\(cqt delay terminal switching
.IP \(bu 2
Produce errors that should prevent terminal switching
.SS \f[CR]session_init\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: In terminal session after switching to worktree
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Execution Context\f[R]: Terminal session (pasted/typed into
terminal)
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Virtual environment activation, shell configuration
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
session_init = \(dq\(dq\(dq
source .env
conda activate myproject
export DEV_MODE=true
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]session_init\f[R] hook is special\(emit\(cqs the only hook
that runs \f[B]inside the new terminal session\f[R].
While other lifecycle hooks run inside the initial
\f[CR]autowt create\f[R] process, \f[CR]session_init\f[R] scripts are
literally pasted/typed into the terminal using terminal automation
(i.e.\ AppleScript).
This allows \f[CR]session_init\f[R] scripts to: activate virtual
environments or start interactive processes.
.SS \f[CR]pre_cleanup\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: Before any cleanup operations begin
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Resource cleanup
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
pre_cleanup = \(dq\(dq\(dq
# Release allocated ports
\&./scripts/release\-ports.sh $AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR]post_cleanup\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: After worktrees and branches are removed
\f[B]Execution Context\f[R]: Subprocess in main repository directory
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Volume cleanup, global state updates
.PP
The \f[CR]post_cleanup\f[R] hook runs in the \f[B]main repository
directory\f[R] (not the worktree directory, which has been deleted).
However, the \f[CR]AUTOWT_WORKTREE_DIR\f[R] environment variable is
still set to the path where the worktree was located.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_cleanup = \(dq\(dq\(dq
# Clean up docker volumes
docker volume rm ${AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME}_db_data 2>/dev/null || true
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR]pre_switch\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: Before switching away from current worktree
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Stop services, save state
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
pre_switch = \(dq\(dq\(dq
# Stop development server
pkill \-f \(dqnpm run dev\(dq || true

# Save current state
\&./scripts/save\-session\-state.sh
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR]post_switch\f[R]
\f[B]Timing\f[R]: After switching to new worktree
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Use cases\f[R]: Start services, restore state
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_switch = \(dq\(dq\(dq
# Start development server in background
nohup npm run dev > dev.log 2>&1 &

# Restore session state
\&./scripts/restore\-session\-state.sh
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SH Terminal support
.SS autowt automates your terminal by default
\f[CR]autowt\f[R]\(cqs intended user experience is that it will open
terminal tabs on your behalf.
It uses \c
.UR https://github.com/irskep/automate-terminal
\f[CR]automate\-terminal\f[R]
.UE \c
\ to accomplish this, so check that project out to find out if your
terminal is supported.
.SS What to do if your terminal isn\(cqt supported or you don\(cqt want this behavior
Add this to your \f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R] or set it at the user level
with \f[CR]autowt config\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
[terminal]
mode = \(aqecho\(aq
.EE
.PP
This will cause autowt to print commands to the console instead of
having your terminal run them automatically.
You can then manually run the printed commands to navigate to the
worktree.
.SS Shell integration (alternative to terminal automation)
Instead of opening new tabs or windows, you can have \f[CR]autowt\f[R]
change directories directly in your current shell.
This is useful if your terminal isn\(cqt supported by
\f[CR]automate\-terminal\f[R], or if you prefer staying in one session.
.PP
For mise installs, shell integration also defines the shorter
\f[CR]awt\f[R] command.
Homebrew installs both \f[CR]autowt\f[R] and \f[CR]awt\f[R] directly.
.PP
Add the appropriate line to your shell config:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# \(ti/.bashrc\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init bash)\(dq

\f[I]# \(ti/.zshrc\f[R]
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init zsh)\(dq

\f[I]# \(ti/.config/fish/config.fish\f[R]
autowt shell\-init fish \f[B]|\f[R] source
.EE
.PP
The shell argument can be omitted if \f[CR]$SHELL\f[R] matches your
config file (i.e., your login shell is the same as the shell you\(cqre
configuring).
When in doubt, pass it explicitly.
.PP
Once configured, running \f[CR]autowt my\-branch\f[R] will \f[CR]cd\f[R]
into the worktree in your current shell instead of opening a new tab or
window.
No terminal automation (AppleScript, tmux, etc.)
is required, so this works in any terminal.
.PP
Commands that don\(cqt switch worktrees (\f[CR]ls\f[R],
\f[CR]cleanup\f[R], \f[CR]config\f[R], etc.)
continue to work normally.
.PP
To preview what the shell function would do without actually changing
directories:
.IP
.EX
eval \(dq$(autowt shell\-init \-\-dry\-run)\(dq
autowt my\-branch  \f[I]# prints the cd command instead of running it\f[R]
.EE
.SH Troubleshooting
Tip: \(lqGetting Help\(rq Before filing an issue, please run your command with the \-\-debug flag to get the full output, which is very helpful for diagnosing problems.
.IP
.EX
autowt \-\-debug <command\-that\-is\-failing>
.EE
.SS Common issues
.SS Terminal control on macOS
\f[B]Symptom\f[R]: \f[CR]autowt\f[R] doesn\(cqt open a new terminal
tab/window, or it fails to switch to an existing session on macOS.
.PP
This is almost always a permissions issue.
\f[CR]autowt\f[R] uses AppleScript to control your terminal, which
requires you to grant access.
.PP
\f[B]Solution\f[R]:
.IP "1." 3
Open \f[CR]System Settings\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Navigate to \f[CR]Privacy & Security\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
Grant your terminal application (e.g., \f[CR]iTerm.app\f[R],
\f[CR]Terminal.app\f[R]) permissions for both \f[B]Accessibility\f[R]
and \f[B]Automation\f[R].
.SH Branch Names from GitHub Issues
Use the GitHub CLI (\f[CR]gh\f[R]) to turn an issue number into a branch
name.
\f[CR]autowt\f[R]\(cqs \f[CR]branch_name\f[R] field runs the command,
captures stdout, and normalizes it into a valid Git ref (lowercase,
dashes, no spaces).
.SS Example \f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts.custom.ghissue]\f[R]
description = \(dqCreate a branch that matches the GitHub issue title\(dq
branch_name = \(dqgh issue view $1 \-\-json number,title \-\-template \(aq{{.number}}\-{{.title}}\(aq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Now \f[CR]autowt ghissue 1234\f[R] asks GitHub for issue
\f[CR]#1234\f[R], builds a string like \f[CR]1234\-improve\-login\f[R],
and creates a worktree on that branch.
All other lifecycle hooks (such as \f[CR]session_init\f[R]) continue to
run the same as when you pass a plain branch name.
.SH Branch Names from Linear
Linear already suggests the branch slug it wants to see when you open a
pull request.
The \c
.UR https://github.com/czottmann/linearis
\f[CR]linearis\f[R]
.UE \c
\ CLI exposes that suggestion as the \f[CR]branchName\f[R] field on
every \f[CR]issues read\f[R] response, so you can hand it directly to
\f[CR]autowt\f[R] and get automatic ticket association when the PR
opens.
.SS Example \f[CR].autowt.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts.custom.linear]\f[R]
description = \(dqCreate a branch from Linear\(aqs suggested slug\(dq
branch_name = \(dqlinearis issues read $1 | jq \-r \(aq.branchName // empty\(aq\(dq
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]branchName\f[R] already includes the team key, issue number, and
slug scrubbed for Git usage.
When you run \f[CR]autowt linear ABC\-123\f[R], the command above:
.IP "1." 3
Calls \f[CR]linearis issues read ABC\-123\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
Extracts \f[CR].branchName\f[R] with \f[CR]jq\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
Hands that exact string to autowt, so the worktree is created on
Linear\(cqs suggested branch
.PP
Because Linear watches for that branch name, any PR pushed from the
branch automatically links back to the ticket.
.SH Auto\-Starting Coding Agents
Suppose you want to make it as easy as possible to have an agent like
Claude Code start working on a prompt in its own worktree.
You could do this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts.custom]\f[R]
claude = \(aqclaude \(dq$1\(dq\(aq

\f[I]# the above is equivalent to:\f[R]
\f[B][scripts.custom.claude]\f[R]
session_init = \(aqclaude \(dq$1\(dq\(aq
.EE
.PP
And now you can start it up in a fresh worktree with ease:
.IP
.EX
autowt claude \(aqAdd a tutorial\(aq
.EE
.PP
This will create a fresh worktree in a new terminal on a branch called
\f[CR]add\-a\-tutorial\f[R] (or
\f[CR]yourbranchprefix/add\-a\-tutorial\f[R], if you have a branch
prefix configured) and launch \f[CR]claude\f[R] with the argument
\f[CR]\(dqAdd a tutorial\(dq\f[R].
.SS Integrating coding agents with issue trackers
If you trust a coding agent to take the first shot at solving an issue,
you can set up autowt to automate plugging it into your issue tracker.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts.custom.fix]\f[R]
description = \(dqHave Claude Code fix a GitHub issue and open a PR\(dq
session_init = \(aqclaude \(dqWork on GitHub issue $1. Commit when finished and open a PR. Use the gh command line tool to interact with GitHub.\(dq\(aq
branch_name = \(dqgh issue view $1 \-\-json number,title \-\-template \(aq{{.number}}\-{{.title}}\(aq\(dq
.EE
.SH Copying Untracked Files Like \f[CR].env\f[R]
You can configure autowt to copy important untracked files from your
main worktree into all new worktrees using either the
\f[CR]post_create\f[R] or the \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] hook.
Common files that need to be copied like this include \f[CR].env\f[R],
\f[CR].pem\f[R] files for SSL certificates, and \f[CR].claude\f[R]
directories if you don\(cqt check those into git.
.PP
The features of autowt that make this easy are the
\f[CR]post_create\f[R]/\f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] hooks which run
scripts at worktree creation time, and the
\f[CR]$AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR\f[R] env var, which is available in scripts
for use cases just like this one.
.PP
\f[CR]post_create\f[R] will do the copy \f[I]before\f[R] opening the new
terminal:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dqcp $AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env .env\(dq
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]post_create_async\f[R] will do the copy \f[I]after\f[R] opening
the new terminal, which can be good for expensive operations:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create_async = \(dqcp $AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.env .env\(dq
.EE
.PP
But for copying untracked files, usually you want
\f[CR]post_create\f[R], not \f[CR]post_create_async\f[R].
.SS Multiple files
Use \f[CR]cp \-r\f[R], or \f[CR]rsync\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dqcp \-r $AUTOWT_MAIN_REPO_DIR/.claude .claude\(dq
.EE
.SH Assigning Unique Ports
Each worktree gets a unique port written to \f[CR].env\f[R], avoiding
conflicts when running multiple dev servers.
.SS Calculate port from branch name
Hash the branch name and map it to a port range:
.IP
.EX
BASE_PORT=3000
PORT_RANGE=1000
HASH=$(echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq \f[B]|\f[R] md5sum \f[B]|\f[R] cut \-c1\-4)
OFFSET=$((16#$HASH % $PORT_RANGE))
PORT=$((BASE_PORT + OFFSET))
.EE
.PP
This gives ports between 3000\-3999 based on the branch name.
.SS Write to .env in post_create
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
BASE_PORT=3000
PORT_RANGE=1000
HASH=$(echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq | md5sum | cut \-c1\-4)
OFFSET=$((16#$HASH % $PORT_RANGE))
PORT=$((BASE_PORT + OFFSET))
echo \(dqPORT=$PORT\(dq >> .env
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Complete config
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
BASE_PORT=3000
PORT_RANGE=1000
HASH=$(echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq | md5sum | cut \-c1\-4)
OFFSET=$((16#$HASH % $PORT_RANGE))
PORT=$((BASE_PORT + OFFSET))
echo \(dqPORT=$PORT\(dq >> .env
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Try it out
Create a worktree and start a server.
This example uses Python\(cqs built\-in static file server.
.IP
.EX
autowt my\-feature
source .env
echo \(dqServer running on port $PORT\(dq
python3 \-m http.server $PORT
.EE
.PP
Each worktree will get a consistent port based on its branch name.
.SH Starting and Stopping Docker Containers
This example will show how to start and stop a container for each
worktree.
Postgres is used to demonstrate, but it could be anything.
.SS Start container in \f[CR]post_create\f[R]
Use the branch name to create a unique container name and port.
The port calculation is covered in more depth in Assigning a Unique
Port.
.IP
.EX
CONTAINER_NAME=\(dqpg\-$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq
BASE_PORT=5432
PORT_RANGE=1000
HASH=$(echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq \f[B]|\f[R] md5sum \f[B]|\f[R] cut \-c1\-4)
OFFSET=$((16#$HASH % $PORT_RANGE))
PORT=$((BASE_PORT + OFFSET))

docker run \-d \(rs
  \-\-name \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq \(rs
  \-e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres \(rs
  \-p \(dq$PORT:5432\(dq \(rs
  postgres:16

echo \(dqDATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres\(atlocalhost:$PORT/postgres\(dq >> .env
.EE
.SS Stop container in \f[CR]pre_cleanup\f[R]
.IP
.EX
CONTAINER_NAME=\(dqpg\-$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq
docker stop \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq 2>/dev/null \f[B]||\f[R] true
docker rm \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq 2>/dev/null \f[B]||\f[R] true
.EE
.SS Complete config
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# .autowt.toml\f[R]
\f[B][scripts]\f[R]
post_create = \(dq\(dq\(dq
CONTAINER_NAME=\(dqpg\-$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq
BASE_PORT=5432
PORT_RANGE=1000
HASH=$(echo \(dq$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq | md5sum | cut \-c1\-4)
OFFSET=$((16#$HASH % $PORT_RANGE))
PORT=$((BASE_PORT + OFFSET))

docker run \-d \(rs
  \-\-name \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq \(rs
  \-e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres \(rs
  \-p \(dq$PORT:5432\(dq \(rs
  postgres:16

echo \(dqDATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres\(atlocalhost:$PORT/postgres\(dq >> .env
\(dq\(dq\(dq

pre_cleanup = \(dq\(dq\(dq
CONTAINER_NAME=\(dqpg\-$AUTOWT_BRANCH_NAME\(dq
docker stop \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq 2>/dev/null || true
docker rm \(dq$CONTAINER_NAME\(dq 2>/dev/null || true
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.SS Try it out
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Create worktree \- container starts automatically\f[R]
autowt my\-feature

\f[I]# Verify container is running\f[R]
docker ps \f[B]|\f[R] grep pg\-my\-feature

\f[I]# Connect to database\f[R]
source .env
psql \(dq$DATABASE_URL\(dq

\f[I]# Cleanup \- container stops and removes\f[R]
autowt cleanup my\-feature

\f[I]# Verify container is gone\f[R]
docker ps \-a \f[B]|\f[R] grep pg\-my\-feature  \f[I]# should return nothing\f[R]
.EE
.SH AUTHORS
autowt.
